mcommander is built from a release archive created by
packaging/release-source.sh.  The archive deliberately contains mc-version.h:
the upstream file is generated in a Git checkout and would otherwise be
reported as "unknown" by a build outside Git.

This directory carries no version.  debian/changelog is generated by
packaging/prepare.sh from the annotated v* tags, and is listed in .gitignore.
Its entries are the tag messages and its dates are the tag dates, so the same
tag always yields the same source package.  The package carries no epoch: it
is a new source name, and mc6 is retired through Conflicts.

To build the source package for a version, take the release archive and copy
this directory into it.  Building from the Git checkout instead would add the
whole of packaging/ as a patch, and record the absence of mc-version.h:

  packaging/release-source.sh 6.0.3 v6.0.3
  packaging/prepare.sh 6.0.3 dist/mcommander-6.0.3.tar.gz
  tar -xf dist/mcommander-6.0.3.tar.gz -C /tmp
  cp dist/mcommander-6.0.3.tar.gz /tmp/mcommander_6.0.3.orig.tar.gz
  cp -r debian /tmp/mcommander-6.0.3/
  cd /tmp/mcommander-6.0.3 && dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

A version without a tag of its own is a test build: prepare.sh marks its entry
UNRELEASED, and dput refuses such a package.  For a PPA, name the target series
and add a series suffix to the version:

  DEB_DISTRIBUTION=noble DEB_VERSION_SUFFIX='~ubuntu24.04.1' \
      packaging/prepare.sh 6.0.3 dist/mcommander-6.0.3.tar.gz
